!.env.example

# What a documentation build writes beside its own configuration. `api/` is the reference generated from the XML
# comments in `src/`, and `_site/` is the rendered site when docfx is run without an output directory;
# `scripts/build-docs-site.sh` writes under `artifacts/` instead, which is ignored above.
docfx/api/
docfx/_site/

# Instructions somebody writes for their own agent sessions and for nobody else's. Claude Code loads
# `CLAUDE.local.md` from the repository root straight after `CLAUDE.md`, which is where a fork's contributor states
# that they are one; `docs/operations/agent-workflow.md` § The two roles says what belongs in it. Ignored by suffix
# rather than by that one name so the same convention holds for whatever else a harness reads, and so a note that is
# true of one machine cannot arrive in a pull request as an unrelated file — no protected path catches it, because
# `CLAUDE.local.md` is not `CLAUDE.md`.
*.local.md

# The plans and design notes the agent harness writes while a change is being worked out. They record how a change
# was arrived at rather than how the system behaves, which is the one thing `docs/` never carries, and they are
# snapshots that were never meant to stay true. Ignored rather than tracked so the harness can keep writing them
# without the workspace preflight or the diff gate ever seeing them.
docs/superpowers/

# Everything an operator provisions for the Compose deployment. The directories themselves are tracked, because the
# Compose file bind-mounts one of them and a missing bind source is a start-up failure rather than an empty mount;
# nothing inside them ever is.
deploy/compose/secrets/*
!deploy/compose/secrets/mailfathom/
deploy/compose/secrets/mailfathom/*
!deploy/compose/secrets/mailfathom/.gitkeep
# The operator's own copy of the example beside it. The example is tracked; the file MailFathom actually reads is not,
# because it names the mailboxes a person reads.
deploy/compose/config/*.json
deploy/compose/compose.override.yaml

# Agent sandboxes mask developer and editor configuration by bind-mounting an empty file over each
# path, so these appear in the worktree as zero-byte files that `git add -A` will happily stage.
# Listed without a trailing slash because the masked form is a file even where the real one is a
# directory, which is why the `.idea/` and `.vs/` patterns above did not match them.
# None of these is tracked. Should one ever need to be, `git add -f` says so out loud, which is the
# quieter failure of the two: the alternative leaves an empty file reappearing before every commit.
.bash_profile
.bashrc
.zshrc
.zprofile
.profile
.gitconfig
.gitmodules
.ripgreprc
.vscode
.idea
.mcp.json
.claude/agents
.claude/commands
.claude/hooks
.claude/output-styles
.claude/routines
.claude/workflows
.claude/launch.json
.claude/loop.md
.claude/scheduled_tasks.json
.claude/settings.json
